  'Set up the flowsites package locally with the CLI, then open it on localhost using the extracted files as delivered.

npx flowsites add web-20-571

What the CLI does: downloads the prompt ZIP, unpacks it into a new project folder, and leaves the source ready to run. Do not recreate or rewrite the project from scratch — use the extracted files as delivered. Do not invent a React/Next app if the extract is already a complete site (and do not flatten a React/Next app into static HTML if package.json is present).

After the command finishes:
1. cd into the folder it created (named after the prompt slug unless a path was passed).
2. Inspect what was extracted — flowsites packages may be React/Next OR static HTML/CSS/JS:
   - If package.json exists: install dependencies (npm install / pnpm install / bun install / yarn), then start the framework dev server (npm run dev / pnpm dev / bun dev / yarn dev).
   - If there is no package.json (typically index.html + HTML/CSS/JS assets): serve the folder with a static server (e.g. `npx --yes serve .` or `python3 -m http.server`). Prefer a local server over opening index.html via file:// so assets and routing work. Do not run npm install / npm run dev on a static export.
3. Open the localhost URL in the browser so the user sees the project running immediately.

Once localhost is up:
- Keep the delivered structure, styling system, and motion as the baseline.
- Apply the user''s follow-up requests (copy, layout, color, sections, features) as edits on top of these files.
- Prefer small, targeted changes over regenerating the whole site.
- After each meaningful change, keep or restart the local server so the user can review on localhost.',
